# 7b-2 (ticket OPUS-7B2-20260929-01; book §12, Q51): a declaration binds only when it executes, and only forward and
# down.  In br, `int: x` inside the if body is that body's field: it takes the argument's current value 3, the body
# works on it (13) -- and after `end` of the body the name is the argument again (a local is not visible outward).  A
# branch not taken binds nothing.  br(3, 1): 3 * 100 + 13 = 313; br(3, 0): 300.  A binding left active after its body
# gives 1313; one activated statically where the branch is not taken gives 0.  Success is 7.
fn: br (int: x; int: go) int
    int: inner 0
    if: go != 0
        int: x
        x: x + 10
        inner: x
    return: x * 100 + inner
end: br
int: a 0
int: b 0
a: br(3, 1)
b: br(3, 0)
int: ok 64
if: a = 313 && b = 300
    ok: 7
if: a = 1313
    ok: 65
if: b = 0
    ok: 66
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
